course: dictionary
lesson: 2456
title: Dangling and hanging loose
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Dangling means hanging loosely and swinging, often from something above. When you meet a new word, look at the words around it. «A toddler pulls a dangling cable» tells you the cable hangs down loose, so a small hand can grab it.
explain: The clues often show the meaning. «Legs dangling» over a wall means the legs hang down and swing in the air. A dangling cord hangs from a plug instead of lying flat on the table. Ask yourself: what would hang, swing or move here?
explain: Some words have a second meaning. Dangling something in front of someone means holding it out to tempt them. «Keep someone dangling» means keep them waiting without an answer. Read the whole sentence to find which meaning fits.
fact: Dangling means hanging loosely and swinging, often from something above.
fact: A dangling cable or cord can be dangerous, so tell an adult if you see one.
fact: Dangling can also mean holding something out to tempt someone.
fact: «Keep someone dangling» means keep them waiting without an answer.
fact: «Male proboscis monkeys have long dangling noses» means their noses hang down loosely.
exercise: «Earrings dangling from her ears sparkled in the sun.» What does dangling mean here? | Lying flat on the floor | Shiny and new | *Hanging loosely and swinging | Hidden inside a box
exercise: Why should you tell an adult about a dangling cable? | It makes the lights brighter | It means the cable is new | It is a sign of a good tea party | *It could be dangerous
exercise: «She kept him dangling all week before she gave an answer.» What does kept him dangling mean? | *Kept him waiting without an answer | Held him up high in the air | Gave him a reward | Pulled him closer
test: «The boy sat on the wall with his legs dangling over the edge.» Where are his legs? | Tucked under his seat | Lying flat on the wall | Wrapped around a tree | *Hanging loosely over the edge
test: Dangling something in front of someone means what? | Throwing it away | Hiding it from them | *Holding it out to tempt them | Dropping it on the floor
exam: «A boy keeps the kettle cord tucked away, because a dangling cord could pull a full boiling kettle onto someone.» Why does he tuck the cord away? | Because cords are expensive | *So it cannot hang down and pull the kettle | To make the kitchen tidier | To stop the kettle from boiling
